{"bookId":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","moduleId":"6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f","subjectId":"6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329","chapterId":"6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","count":20,"questions":[{"id":309806,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"<p>The correct order of the stability of the following compounds based on hyperconjugation is</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fts_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252Faf8a2fdb-c5b3-494a-b7d7-32b93b88145e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 514px; height: 200px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-aba4a174cd2898c1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-aba4a174cd2898c1.png\"></p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<blockquote><p>Hyperconjugation increases the stability of alkene which does not have any other resonance effect. And hyperconjugation effect depends on the number of alpha hydrogen around the alkene. More the number of alpha hydrogen atoms more stable the alkene will be.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fts_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252F3968d947-6d4f-43ec-9560-d90090952ea0-af8a2fdb-c5b3-494a-b7d7-32b93b88145e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 206px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-da4fb90acd8df1bc.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-da4fb90acd8df1bc.png\"></p><p>So, stability order will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>.</p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309807,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"In Lassaigne's extract of organic compound both nitrogen and sulphur are present, which gives blood-red colour with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> due to the formation of","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mfenced><mi>SCN</mi></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Fe</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><msub><mi>xH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaSCN</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>CN</mi></mfenced><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>NOS</mi></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>When the organic sample containing nitrogen and sulphur is strongly heated with sodium metal the ionic compound sodium thiocyanate is formed. Sodium thiocyanate gives blood-red colour with neutral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>FeCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow></mrow></mover><mi>NaSCN</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><msup><mi>SCN</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>+</mo><mo> </mo><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mover><mo>→</mo><mrow></mrow></mover><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Fe</mi><mfenced><mi>SCN</mi></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309808,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Which of the following does not show geometrical isomerism?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>Cl</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>OH</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>CH</mi><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<blockquote><p>For a compound to show the geometrical isomerism, it should fulfill the following conditions</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math> Bonds should have restricted rotation</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo></math> The groups/substituents which are bonded to restricted atoms must be different</p><p><img height=\"157\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/3c4aff6b-68b3-4f63-a9f6-692c695e7738-image.png\" width=\"344\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-22c21387b7e0e846.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-22c21387b7e0e846.png\"></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309809,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"The stability of carbanions in the following.<br /><br />(i) <math> <mrow> <mtext>RC</mtext> <mo>=</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8854;</mo> </mover> </mrow> </math>&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;  <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/NEET/DPP-Chemistry/EM0020075_Question_1.jpg\" style=\"width: 119px; height: 77px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c1520af3594f8fce.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c1520af3594f8fce.png\"><br /><br />(iii) <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>R</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>=</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8854;</mo> </mover> <mtext>H</mtext> </mrow> </math>  &nbsp; &nbsp;  (iv) <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>R</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mover> <mo>C</mo> <mo>&#8853;</mo> </mover> <mo>=</mo> <mrow> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8854;</mo> </mover> </mrow> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math><br /><br />is in the order of","options":["(iv) &gt; (ii) &gt; (iii) &gt; (i)","(i) &gt; (iii) &gt; (ii) &gt; (iv)","(i) &gt; (ii) &gt; (iii) &gt; (iv)","(ii) &gt; (iii) &gt; (iv) &gt; (i)"],"answer":2,"solution":"Higher the no. of electron releasing group lower will be stability of carbanion, and vice versa. So the order of stability of carbanions is<br /><br /><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/NEET/DPP-Chemistry/EM0020075_AnsExp_1.jpg\" style=\"width: 396px; height: 74px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-45494a932afa4ece.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-45494a932afa4ece.png\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309810,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"<p>The order of basicity among the following compounds is</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/a82ddd10-1c1d-4e19-8583-eff3ec2d5510-image.png\" style=\"width: 415px; height: 328px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-fb816a2388c6af70.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-fb816a2388c6af70.png\">&nbsp;</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>IV</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi></math></p><p>(I) Conjugated acid has three equivalent canonical forms (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;lone pair of&nbsp;electrons on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>-atom).</p><p>(IV) Conjugated acid has two equivalent canonical forms (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;lone pair of electrons on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>- atom).</p><p>(II) (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;lone pair of&nbsp;electrons on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>-atom, not in resonance).</p><p>(III) Conjugated acid has two equivalent canonical forms (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;lone pair of&nbsp;electrons on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>-atom, but in resonance).</p>","difficulty":"Hard","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309811,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"If the number of isomeric forms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;and the number of compounds with the chiral centre is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>, then the ratio of&nbsp; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#39;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>&#39;</mo></math>&nbsp;is (all isomeric forms contain a benzene ring):","options":["<span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span></span></span>","<span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span></span></span>","<span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span></span></span>","<span style=\"font-size:10.0pt\"><span style=\"line-height:107%\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;,serif\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span></span></span>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<blockquote><p>The isomeric forms of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math>&nbsp;are:</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/250ba821-12e1-4b80-9b2e-89e4c0a7076d-image.png\" style=\"width: 144px; height: 129px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ee26365468001ed9.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ee26365468001ed9.png\">&nbsp;<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/755e435c-3108-4a93-8fd0-10509060ecf0-image.png\" style=\"width: 143px; height: 136px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b6f5c7abb80629b9.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b6f5c7abb80629b9.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/e17a3ac1-398c-4836-8900-ba86b38b2f0f-image.png\" style=\"width: 89px; height: 173px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-519f0399429a5930.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-519f0399429a5930.png\">&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/d773c10c-ca9e-4887-b8f7-8e61ca04d41b-image.png\" style=\"width: 147px; height: 146px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f1eb29c6f4566e87.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f1eb29c6f4566e87.png\"></p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/e979c473-0847-46ff-926c-46b570738d5e-image.png\" style=\"width: 178px; height: 133px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-17ee0893a8f2b6c8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-17ee0893a8f2b6c8.png\">&nbsp;</p><p>The&nbsp;number of isomeric forms is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>.</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Chiral centre is the centre of the atom which contains four different valencies. Nitrogen consists of three different groups along with one lone pair. Therefore, the given structure consists of one chiral nitrogen atom.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/5e516dab-0fba-42c3-8fb8-a6dbde0a618b-image.png\" style=\"width: 178px; height: 133px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d00132788fe2ed6a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d00132788fe2ed6a.png\"></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309812,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Which one of the following methods is suitable to separate a mixture of $n$-pentane and toluene?","options":["Steam distillation","Simple distillation","Sublimation","Vacuum distillation"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\because$ Toluene is\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fts_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252F0ZYQGwD3tEaVKLPD8lkm5W5h3Z97ioR2vgVJPCYPf7o.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0de104cece6871b9.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0de104cece6871b9.png\"><br>\r\n$n$-pentane ion $\\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{CH}_2-\\mathrm{CH}_2-\\mathrm{CH}_2-\\mathrm{CH}_3$ one is aromatic and other is straight chain compound. Therefore, it can be separated by simple distillation.","difficulty":"Easy","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309813,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Match the following.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252F99_percentile_module%252FBet3GHpbcQSnqrynm2X4M6fyQqhRUrZ1gESUI7AoH_I.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bae0c842c3743729.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bae0c842c3743729.png\"><br/>","options":["$\\begin{array}{llll}1 &amp; 4 &amp; 3 &amp; 5\\end{array}","$\\begin{array}{llll}2 &amp; 4 &amp; 1 &amp; 5\\end{array}","$\\begin{array}{llll}5 &amp; 1 &amp; 4 &amp; 2\\end{array}","$\\begin{array}{llll}5 &amp; 1 &amp; 3 &amp; 2\\end{array}"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252F99_percentile_module%252FeygnxPYnq8oiUCSAuUlfvqksdbqvqlyIlE-JIHC3C-o.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c7442b14fd5bff72.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c7442b14fd5bff72.png\"><br/>\nThus, these two are tautomers.\nEclipsed and staggered ethane are two conformations of ethane.\n(+)2-butanol and (-)2-butanol are enantiomers as these are non-superimposable mirror images.<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252F99_percentile_module%252FOiXdgvv_zteoU4htyC0COYPoO8qPyfXruP8exQsUPHw.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7d5639a3b2250e21.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets