nti, gauche) of ethylene glycol is :","options":["Eclipse&lt; gauche&lt; anti","Gauche&lt;eclips&lt; anti","Eclipse&lt; anti&lt; gauche","Anti&lt; gauche&lt; eclipse"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p><math> <mrow> <munder> <mrow> <mtext>C</mtext> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mrow> <mover> <mtext>O</mtext> <mtext>|</mtext> </mover> <mtext>H</mtext> </mrow> </munder> <mo>-</mo> <munder> <mrow> <mtext>C</mtext> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> <mover> <mrow> <mtext>O</mtext> <mtext>H</mtext> </mrow> <mtext>|</mtext> </mover> </munder> </mrow> </math><br /><br />&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/75f0c1df-d6ac-48bc-8b2c-e1cd601ed99e-image.png\" style=\"width: 499px; height: 177px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b8317dcd960cc547.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b8317dcd960cc547.png\"></p><p>in gauche intramolecular H -bonding is taking place so it is highly stable among all.in staggered&nbsp; anti steric repulsion is less as eclipsed so it is more stable than eclipsed&nbsp;<br /><math> <mrow> <mn>Stability order &#8658; Gauche &#62; Anti &#62; Eclipse</mn> </mrow> </math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309816,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Tropolone is","options":["benzenoid and aromatic","non-benzenoid and not aromatic","non-benzenoid and aromatic","non-benzenoid and anti-aromatic"],"answer":2,"solution":"Tropolone is\r\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fts_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252F-LxE-aEgdkuXP676_71WU3jbvCsJIidF0Vd_eBJ_iVE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1541ff7f7d4d039b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1541ff7f7d4d039b.png\"><br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\\text { Tropolone is non-benzenoid quasi aromatics. }\r\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309817,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"<p>The correct order of acidity of the following is</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fap_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252F5c7a881e-d37e-4828-a5cd-88026a310821-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 163px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7f9e057a589ca12d.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7f9e057a589ca12d.png\"></p><p>&nbsp;</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>III</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>III</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mo>&#62;</mo><mi>II</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<blockquote><p>The electron withdrawing groups increases the acidic strength of phenols and the electron releasing groups decreases the acidic strength of phenols. The methyl group and methoxy groups are electron releasing groups at para position. Hence, both p-cresol and p-methoxy phenol are less acidic than phenol. Methoxy group is electron releasing group by +M effect and methyl group is hyperconjugation. Hence, p-methoxy pheniol is less acidic than p-cresol. The correct acidic strength order is</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fap_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252Fdc6271da-bbbe-4af7-8d6e-67830cb8caa1-5c7a881e-d37e-4828-a5cd-88026a310821-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 219px; height: 147px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8d4d69c64fd2b34c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8d4d69c64fd2b34c.png\"></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309818,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Which of the following compound does not exhibit geometrical Isomerism?","options":["2-Butene","3-Hexene","But-2-enal","Styrene"],"answer":3,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fap_eamcet%252Fpy35bdsq%252FtxwV8r0kw3Ul4NiaAJ-3rCKQnxaHU9CseRRTP60REMI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d73280d29cd586a4.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d73280d29cd586a4.png\"><br>\r\nStyrene does not exhibit geometrical isomerism as it has three identical groups (H-atoms) on the doubly-bonded carbon atoms.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309819,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Which of the following compound(s) has ' $Z$ ' configuration?\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/bYV68CdI68Ro2TofhzUhAN4zwLUtKQcFS4DjcYMHILw.original.fullsize.png\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6ff4b3f9cec1e5ca.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6ff4b3f9cec1e5ca.png\"><br/>","options":["(i) only","(ii) only","(iii) only","(i) and (iii)"],"answer":3,"solution":"When the groups with higher priority (ie, with high atomic number) are present on same side of double bond, then the configuration is $Z$ but when present on opposite side of double bond, the configuration is $E$.\n<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/4WR3soEBj-CcBD2841J9k6cVkCcSyi-QWr93-09QiNk.original.fullsize.png\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2787a1abc1d00d64.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-2787a1abc1d00d64.png\"><br/>\n(Priority : $\\mathrm{Br}&gt;\\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_3&gt;\\mathrm{H}$ )\nHence, compound (i) and (iii) have $(Z)$ configuration.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309820,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Which compound amongst the following is not an aromatic compound?","options":["<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Fe7d4f485-650a-4709-a4b1-71a5788d007e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 76px; height: 80px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9393b9b5e28b4a46.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9393b9b5e28b4a46.png\">   </p>","<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252F630857d7-70d7-462b-ba61-ce7818331d21-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 50px; height: 55px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c097ac7c986483e8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c097ac7c986483e8.png\"></p><p> </p>","<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Ff27b0666-4fb9-4ed6-b8b9-8a6d4ce5284d-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 83px; height: 80px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-47217e75fc3970be.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-47217e75fc3970be.png\">   </p>","<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Fb24d649f-c932-4934-b710-b640659fb5d5-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 78px; height: 85px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-64073ce4b683a88c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-64073ce4b683a88c.png\">   </p>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p> German chemist and physicist Erich Hückel proposed a theory to help determine if a planar ring molecule would have aromatic properties. His rule states that <b>if a cyclic, planar molecule has 4n+2 π electrons, it is considered aromatic</b>. This rule would come to be known as Hückel's Rule</p><p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Ff27b0666-4fb9-4ed6-b8b9-8a6d4ce5284d-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 104px; height: 100px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-47217e75fc3970be.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-47217e75fc3970be.png\">   </p><p>In the above molecule,There is an sp<sup>3</sup> hybridized carbon in the ring.Hence, it is not involved in conjugation. As delocalization of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mo> </mo></math>electrons is not there throughout the ring.It is not an aromatic species.</p><p>It does not follow Huckel's rule.</p><p>But in the other options the species given that is <img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Fe7d4f485-650a-4709-a4b1-71a5788d007e-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 95px; height: 100px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9393b9b5e28b4a46.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9393b9b5e28b4a46.png\">,<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252Fb24d649f-c932-4934-b710-b640659fb5d5-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 92px; height: 100px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-64073ce4b683a88c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-64073ce4b683a88c.png\">and<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fquizrr%252Fquestion-assets%252Fneet%252F2025%252Fpyqsd64af1fs5%252F630857d7-70d7-462b-ba61-ce7818331d21-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 64px; height: 70px;\"/ data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c097ac7c986483e8.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c097ac7c986483e8.png\">are aromatic as they obey Huckel's rule</p><p> </p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309821,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Among the following four compounds\r\nA. Phenol\r\nB. Methyl phenol\r\nC. meta-nitrophenol\r\nD. para nitrophenol\r\nthe acidity order is","options":["D $&gt;$ C $&gt;$ A $&gt;$ B","C $&gt;$ D $&gt;$ A $&gt;$ B","A $&gt;$ D $&gt;$ C $&gt;$ B","B $&gt;$ A $&gt;$ C $&gt;$ D"],"answer":0,"solution":"An electron withdrawing group (-I showing group like $\\left.-\\mathrm{NO}_2,-\\mathrm{CN}\\right)$ stabilises the phenoxide ion, thus when present, increases the acidity of phenol. On the other hand electron releasing groups ($+I$ showing group like $-\\mathrm{CH}_3,-\\mathrm{C}_2 \\mathrm{H}_5$), when present, decrease the acidity of phenol by destabilising phenoxide ion. Hence, the correct order of acidity of given compounds is\r\n\\(\\underset{(D)}{\\text{p-nitrophenol}}  &gt;  \\underset{(C)}{\\text{m-nitrophenol}}  &gt;  \\underset{(A)}{\\text{phenol}}  &gt;  \\underset{(B)}{\\text{methyl phenol}}\\)<br>\r\n(due to intermolecular H -bonding)","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309822,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"Decreasing order of stability of given carbocations is?<br><br>(1) <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/NEET/DPP-Chemistry/EM0067125_Question.jpg\" style=\"width: 66px; height: 83px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9723835b66f62eff.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9723835b66f62eff.png\">  (2)  <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>⊕</mo> </mover> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math><br><br>(3) <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>C</mtext> <mn>6</mn> </msub> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>5</mn> </msub> <mo>-</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>⊕</mo> </mover> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>  (4)  <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mo>-</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>⊕</mo> </mover> <mtext>H</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>","options":["3 &gt; 2 &gt; 4 &gt; 1","1 &gt; 3 &gt; 4 &gt; 2","1 &gt; 3 &gt;2 &gt; 4","3 &gt; 2 &gt; 1 &gt; 4"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>The factors affecting carbanion stability:</p><p>1. If carbanion is on more percentage&nbsp;s character carbon,&nbsp;it will be more stable.</p><p>2. If carbanion is attached to any electron&nbsp;withdrawing group (groups those show negative inductive, mesomeric and hyperconjugation effect), its stability increases.</p><p>3. If carbanion is busy in aromatic stability, it will still be more stable than any other factor.</p><p>Preference of effects:&nbsp;</p><p>Aromaticity &gt; resonance &gt; mesomeric &gt; hyperconjugation &gt; inductive</p><p>(1)  <img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/NEET/DPP-Chemistry/EM0067125_Question.jpg\" style=\"width: 66px; height: 83px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9723835b66f62eff.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9723835b66f62eff.png\"> <span style=\"font-size: 13px;\">Aromatic</span> <br /><br />(2)  <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> <mo>=</mo> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8853;</mo> </mover> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn>&#160;</msub></mrow></math>(2 Resonating Structure )<br /><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;  <br><br>(3)<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/NEET/DPP-Chemistry/cbdducac.jpg\" style=\"width: 186px; height: 180px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-fe338da2cca06a34.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-fe338da2cca06a34.png\"> <span style=\"font-size: 13px;\"> <math> <mrow>&#160;<mover><mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8853;</mo> </mover> <msub> <mtext>H</mtext> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;more delocalised (4 Resonating Structure )&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;<br /><br />&nbsp; (4)  <math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> <mo>-</mo> <mover> <mtext>C</mtext> <mo>&#8853;</mo> </mover> <mtext>H</mtext> <mo>-</mo> <msub> <mtext>CH</mtext> <mn>3</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> (6 hyper conjugation )<br /><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;  </span></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309823,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"To which process aniline and water can be separated by","options":["continuous extraction","column chromatography","thin layer chromatography","none of these"],"answer":0,"solution":"<blockquote><p>Continuous extraction&nbsp;is a separation process which consists in distilling water together with other volatile&nbsp;components. The continuous extraction process works on the principle that when a mixture of two or more undissolved liquids are heated, while ensuring that the surfaces of both liquids are in contact with the atmosphere, the vapor pressure exerted by the system increases. This is because it now becomes the sum of the vapor pressures of all of the components of the mixture combined together. This allows for evaporation of elements with high boiling points at much lower temperatures merely by allowing them to form a mixture with water. Aniline is separated from a mixture by using this method.</p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309824,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"An aromatic compound of formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></math> has&nbsp;how many isomers?","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<blockquote><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math> isomers.</p><p>In aryl halides, the position of chlorine group is changed to give ortho, meta and para form and the last isomer is alkyl halide.</p><p>In the&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>th</mi></msup></math> isomer,&nbsp;the main chain is an aliphatic part and phenyl is treated as a side chain. So, this is a type of alkyl halide.</p><p><img height=\"114\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool..app/backlog/918c5f13-6b42-4f93-b9b8-8ff5ff20544d-image.png\" width=\"473\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6d15d076cb8e300a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6d15d076cb8e300a.png\"></p></blockquote>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"},{"id":309825,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"General Organic Chemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","q":"$0.16 \\mathrm{~g}$ of an organic compound containing sulphur produces $0.233 \\mathrm{~g}$ of $\\mathrm{BaSO}_4$. Percentage of sulphur in the compound is","options":["20","80","50","10"],"answer":0,"solution":"Percentage of S\n$\\begin{aligned}\n&amp; =\\frac{32 \\times \\text { weight of } \\mathrm{BaSO}_4 \\times 100}{233 \\times \\text { weight of compound }} \\\\\n&amp; =\\frac{32 \\times 0.233 \\times 100}{233 \\times 0.16} \\\\\n&amp; =20\n\\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Easy","source":"Highly selective Backlog Qs for JEE Main","_book":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be","_chapterKey":"6894d29d3156b1f3ca5ad0be__6894d2f5d0af19a8bc64156f__6894d32d684a1fba0f80f329__6894dc01c9600b95aa6a5c80"}]}